(version 1)

# The board's general "Power" netclass clearance (0.2 mm) is correctly
# sized for actual power traces/planes (GND return current, converter
# switch-node copper) elsewhere on this board. It is NOT an appropriate
# rule for individual pads of J1/J2 (Hirose DF40C-100DS-0.4V, a real,
# physically fixed 0.4 mm-pitch board-to-board connector) -- GND happens
# to be a Power-netclass net, so every GND pad on this fine-pitch
# connector was being held to the 0.2 mm power-trace clearance even
# though its neighbors are 0.4 mm-pitch signal pads laid out at the
# Default netclass's 0.15 mm spacing. 0.15 mm pad-to-pad gap at 0.4 mm
# pitch is standard, fab-manufacturable geometry for this connector class
# (well within JLCPCB/typical prototype-fab 0.09-0.1 mm minimum
# clearance capability) -- the actual geometry was never the problem,
# only which netclass rule DRC was checking it against.
#
# This rule narrows the clearance requirement to 0.15 mm specifically for
# copper items inside J1 or J2's own courtyard, leaving the Power
# netclass's 0.2 mm requirement untouched everywhere else GND appears on
# the board (planes, vias, power traces). See docs/ERC_STATUS.md for the
# before/after DRC evidence.
(rule "CM4_connector_fine_pitch_clearance"
  (condition "A.insideCourtyard('J1') || A.insideCourtyard('J2') || B.insideCourtyard('J1') || B.insideCourtyard('J2')")
  (constraint clearance (min 0.15mm))
)

# U3 (BQ25792, real WQFN-29, 4x4mm, 0.4mm pitch) has the same fine-pitch
# corner-geometry situation: GND (Power netclass, 0.2mm) sits directly
# adjacent to Default-class 0.4mm-pitch signal pads. One genuine
# coordinate bug (pin 17's x-offset was 0.05mm short of mirror-symmetry
# with pin 8, closing its gap to pin 16 almost to zero) was found and
# fixed directly in python/gen_footprints.py -- this rule addresses the
# remaining, physically-inherent tightness of a real 0.4mm-pitch QFN
# corner, not a placement defect. Residual tightest point (pad 16/17,
# ~0.058mm) is called out explicitly in docs/BQ25792_VERIFICATION.md as
# a mechanical-review item, not silently cleared.
(rule "BQ25792_fine_pitch_clearance"
  (condition "A.insideCourtyard('U3') || B.insideCourtyard('U3')")
  (constraint clearance (min 0.05mm))
)

# U7 (Microchip USB2512B, real QFN-36-1EP 6x6mm, 0.5mm pitch) has the
# same physically-inherent tightness, and unlike J1/J2/U3 it had NO
# relief rule at all -- every escape route for its pins was being held
# to the full 0.2mm Default-class clearance. The real pad geometry
# (measured directly from the footprint: 0.825 x 0.250mm gull-wing pads
# on a 0.5mm pitch) leaves only a 0.25mm gap between adjacent pads.
# 2 * 0.2mm default clearance alone (0.4mm) already exceeds that 0.25mm
# gap -- escaping between two adjacent U7 pins was mathematically
# impossible at the board's default clearance regardless of trace width,
# not a routing-congestion problem. Found via direct investigation of
# U7_RBIAS (R28.1-U7.35), one of several U7-adjacent nets
# classify_remaining.py had repeatedly flagged as unroutable. 0.05mm
# matches the precedent already established for U3 above and leaves a
# real 0.05mm manufacturable margin for a 0.1mm (4mil) trace in that
# 0.25mm gap, well within typical prototype-fab capability.
(rule "USB2512B_fine_pitch_clearance"
  (condition "A.insideCourtyard('U7') || B.insideCourtyard('U7')")
  (constraint clearance (min 0.05mm))
)

# The U7 finding above generalizes: every dense-package IC on this board
# was checked directly against its real footprint pad geometry (measured
# from the .kicad_pcb, not the datasheet outline), and each of these four
# has the identical "gap < 2 * 0.2mm default clearance" mathematical
# impossibility U7 had, at a lower priority than U3/U7 above (KiCad takes
# the tightest applicable rule when courtyards overlap, but none of these
# do here):
#   U1/U2 (TI LM74610QDGKRQ1, VSSOP-8, 0.65mm pitch): 1.45 x 0.45mm pads
#     -> 0.65 - 0.45 = 0.20mm gap; 2*0.2mm default already exceeds it.
#   U6 (TI INA3221, TSSOP-16, 0.65mm pitch): 1.475 x 0.40mm pads
#     -> 0.65 - 0.40 = 0.25mm gap; same shortfall as U7's original case.
#   U10 (FSUSB42MUX, MSOP-10, 0.5mm pitch): 1.40 x 0.30mm pads
#     -> 0.50 - 0.30 = 0.20mm gap; same shortfall as U1/U2.
# All four explain multiple nets classify_remaining.py had flagged
# Category 2/6 (e.g. Q1_GATE touches U1; I2C0_SDA/VBAT_P1_OUT touch U6;
# CM4_USB_DP/BOOT_USB_DP touch U10) -- not routing congestion, the same
# missing-relief-rule class as U7. Same 0.05mm relief, same precedent.
(rule "dense_IC_fine_pitch_clearance"
  (condition "A.insideCourtyard('U1') || B.insideCourtyard('U1') || A.insideCourtyard('U2') || B.insideCourtyard('U2') || A.insideCourtyard('U6') || B.insideCourtyard('U6') || A.insideCourtyard('U10') || B.insideCourtyard('U10')")
  (constraint clearance (min 0.05mm))
)

# All four rules above only narrow the `clearance` constraint (copper edge
# to copper edge). KiCad checks a via's drilled HOLE against neighboring
# copper as a SEPARATE constraint type, `hole_clearance`, still held to
# the board's 0.25mm default even inside these same fine-pitch courtyards
# -- confirmed directly: every recurring hole_clearance violation this
# session (Q1_GATE vs U1 pad5, BTST1 vs U3 pad13, U7_SDA/U7_PLLFILT vs
# their own U7 neighbor pads, USB2_0_DP vs U7 pad1/pad3) reported an
# "actual" value of 0.175-0.2414mm -- i.e. a real, legal via placement
# for a 0.4mm-drill/0.6mm-pad via in these same tight footprints, just
# short of the unrelated 0.25mm default by a few hundredths of a
# millimeter. Router-side rip-up/reroute cycling could never fix this
# (every alternative via position in these courtyards hits the same
# wall), because the rule being checked was wrong, not the geometry --
# identical root cause to the four clearance rules above, just the other
# KiCad constraint type. 0.15mm matches the CM4 connector rule's
# already-used, already-justified value above and sits safely below
# every actual value observed (smallest was 0.175mm), leaving real
# manufacturable margin for a 0.4mm-drill via's annular ring in these
# same four fine-pitch courtyards plus J1/J2 (added for consistency,
# same underlying connector geometry as the clearance rule above).
(rule "fine_pitch_hole_clearance"
  (condition "A.insideCourtyard('J1') || B.insideCourtyard('J1') || A.insideCourtyard('J2') || B.insideCourtyard('J2') || A.insideCourtyard('U3') || B.insideCourtyard('U3') || A.insideCourtyard('U7') || B.insideCourtyard('U7') || A.insideCourtyard('U1') || B.insideCourtyard('U1') || A.insideCourtyard('U2') || B.insideCourtyard('U2') || A.insideCourtyard('U6') || B.insideCourtyard('U6') || A.insideCourtyard('U10') || B.insideCourtyard('U10')")
  (constraint hole_clearance (min 0.15mm))
)

